course: biology
lesson: 372
title: Ukraine's forests: bison, elk, lynx, wolves and Carpathian brown bears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Deep in a Ukrainian reserve a huge dark shape stands among the trees, as heavy as a small car: a European bison, zubr in Ukrainian, the heaviest land animal in Europe.
explain: A hundred years ago the bison died out in the wild, and every bison alive today comes from just 12 animals kept in zoos. Bringing a species back is possible, but it leaves it with very little variety, so one illness could hit them all.
explain: Ukraine's forests in Polissia and the Carpathians also hide giant elk wading in bogs, shy lynx with black ear tufts, wolves, a few hundred brown bears, turkey-sized capercaillies and black and yellow fire salamanders.
explain: Big plant-eaters keep clearings open, hunters keep plant-eaters moving, and even a fallen dead beech is full of life: beetles, fungi and woodpeckers live in it. A forest works like a team.
fact: The European bison, zubr in Ukrainian, is the heaviest land animal in Europe; a big bull weighs about 900 kilograms.
fact: It died out in the wild in the 1920s and was bred back from zoo animals, and every European bison today comes from just 12 animals.
fact: European bison eat grass, leaves, bark and twigs and keep forest clearings open; herds live in reserves in Polissia, the Carpathians and other parts of Ukraine.
fact: European bison are calmer than American bison but are still dangerous if people come too close.
fact: The elk, called a moose in America, is the biggest deer in the world; males grow huge flat antlers each summer and drop them each winter, a pair sometimes about 20 kilograms.
fact: Elk are good swimmers and eat water plants as well as twigs.
fact: The Eurasian lynx lives in the Carpathians and Polissia, is rare and protected, has big furry snowshoe paws, a short stubby tail and two black ear tufts, and hunts hares and roe deer.
fact: Ukraine has a few thousand wolves, which hunt deer and boar and usually avoid people; in the folk tale Sirko an old dog and a wolf become friends.
fact: A few hundred brown bears live in the Ukrainian Carpathians, and near Lake Synevyr a centre cares for bears rescued from cages, circuses and restaurants.
fact: Rescued bears can rarely go back to the wild because they never learned to look after themselves.
fact: On bear trails people talk or sing so a bear hears them and moves away, pack food tightly and never feed bears; a bear standing on its back legs is usually just looking and smelling.
fact: The capercaillie, hlukhar or «the deaf one» in Ukrainian, is the biggest grouse; for a moment in its clicking spring song it seems not to hear, and it is now rare.
fact: The Ukrainian Carpathians have some of Europe's biggest old beech forests never cut by people, part of a World Heritage Site across several countries.
fact: The fire salamander lives in damp Carpathian forests; the old belief that it lives in fire probably came from salamanders crawling out of logs thrown on a fire, and its bright colours warn of poisonous skin.
exercise: What is the heaviest land animal in Europe? | The European bison | The Carpathian brown bear | The elk of the northern woods | The heavy wild boar
exercise: Why is it risky that all European bison come from just 12 animals? | There is little variety, so one illness or weakness can hit them all | Twelve bison are too heavy for one zoo | They can only eat the plants that the first twelve animals ate in the zoo | They will all be exactly the same size
exercise: What do European bison eat in the forest? | Grass, leaves, bark and twigs | Fish and frogs from the rivers | Deer, hares and other small animals | Only mushrooms and wild berries
exercise: How do bison help a forest? | Their grazing keeps clearings open | They plant trees with their horns | They eat the wolves | They dig ponds for frogs
exercise: What is the biggest deer in the world? | The elk, called a moose in America | The red deer of the Carpathian forests | The roe deer | The reindeer
exercise: What happens to a male elk's antlers every winter? | They drop off and new ones grow next summer | They turn white like the snow around him | They keep growing all year | They fall off only when he gets very old and weak
exercise: Which forest cat has black ear tufts and a short stubby tail? | The Eurasian lynx | The little wild cat | The spotted leopard | The snow leopard of the high mountains
exercise: How do a lynx's big furry paws help it? | They work like snowshoes on deep snow | They help it dig deep burrows | They help it swim fast | They make a loud drumming sound when it runs
exercise: Who are the friends in the Ukrainian folk tale Sirko? | An old dog and a wolf | A fox and a hare | A bear and a goat | A cat and a rooster
exercise: Why can a bear raised in a cage rarely be set free? | It never learned to find food and look after itself | Its fur falls out in the wild and it freezes | Wild bears always welcome it | It forgets how to walk
exercise: Why do people talk or sing on bear trails? | So a bear hears them and moves away | So bears come out and dance to the songs | To scare away the mosquitoes and midges | Because bears like music and come to listen
exercise: A bear stands up on its back legs. What is it usually doing? | Looking and smelling | Getting ready to attack | Dancing to music it hears | Showing that it is tired
exercise: What does hlukhar, the Ukrainian name of the capercaillie, mean? | The deaf one | The loud one | The black one | The fast one
exercise: Why is a fallen dead beech full of life? | Beetles, fungi and woodpeckers feed and live in it | It grows new leaves and roots from the ground | Fish hide under it | It stays alive and keeps growing for many more years
exercise: Why might people long ago have thought salamanders were born in fire? | Salamanders crawled out of logs thrown on a fire | Salamanders glow red in the dark like hot coals | Salamanders eat hot coals | Salamanders are hot to touch
exercise: What do a fire salamander's bright black and yellow colours mean? | Its skin makes poison, so look but do not touch | It is tasty, so birds come from far away to eat it | It is a baby that will turn green | It is cold and needs sun
test: Count them: every European bison alive today comes from how many animals? | 12 | 2 | 120 | 1,200
test: Count them: a lynx sits on a snowy rock. How many black ear tufts and how many tails? | 2 tufts and 1 short tail | 4 tufts and 1 long tail | 2 tufts and 2 tails | 1 tuft and 1 short tail
test: Count them: a camera trap shows 3 wolves. How many legs? | 12 | 9 | 6 | 16
test: «a bison's just a big cow, i'll go get a selfie close up» What is right? | Stay back, bison are calm but still dangerous up close | Bison are tame like cows, so go ahead and stand right next to it | Bison are scared of phones | Only American bison can hurt people
test: In The Jungle Book Baloo the bear is cuddly and loves to be hugged. What is right about real bears? | Real bears are wild and dangerous and must never be fed or approached | Real bears love hugs from children and only bite grown-ups who tease them | Real bears only eat honey | Real bears are afraid of all people
test: What would happen if hikers walked silently and surprised a bear on a narrow path? | A startled bear might defend itself, worse than if it heard them coming | The bear would always fall asleep | Nothing, bears never notice people walking past | The bear would follow them home as a pet and live in their garden
test: «there's a giant cow with a beard flap swimming in the bog lol» What animal is it most likely? | An elk | A European bison | A wild boar | A wolf
test: Why is a lynx almost never seen even where it lives? | It is rare, shy and hunts quietly in thick forest | It only comes out of its den once a year, in spring | It lives only in deep caves high up in the mountains | Its fur makes it completely invisible all summer long
test: «wolves are always the bad guys, every story says so» What is right? | In the tale Sirko a wolf helps an old dog, and real wolves usually avoid people | Wolves always attack people on sight, and that is why every story warns us | Wolves are extinct in Ukraine | Wolves only live in fairy tales
test: In a cartoon a deer keeps the same antlers its whole life. What is right for an elk? | It drops its antlers each winter and grows new ones | It keeps the same antlers for life, adding one point a year | Its antlers are made of wood | Only females grow antlers
test: Why would a forest need big plant-eaters like bison to stay healthy? | Their grazing keeps clearings open, so more kinds of plants and animals live | They eat all the old trees so none are left | They stop the rain from falling | They keep the wolves fat, so the wolves never need to hunt any other animals
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the European bison possesses limited genetic variety at present. | The entire present population is descended from merely twelve animals | It has interbred with domestic cattle exclusively for many centuries | It reproduces only once in a century | It was introduced from America in recent decades
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the antlers of the male elk. | They are grown each summer and shed each winter | They are retained throughout the life of the animal | They are borne solely by the female | They are composed of wood
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct recommended to persons traversing territory inhabited by brown bears? | One ought to talk or sing so that a bear perceives one's approach | One ought to proceed in absolute silence so as not to disturb the bears | One ought to offer food to any bear encountered | One ought to run upon sighting a bear
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a bear reared in captivity can seldom be released into the wild. | It never acquired the skills required to sustain itself | It is unable to tolerate the climate of forests | Wild bears invariably adopt it | Captivity renders bears unable to walk
